flt5PMHijrqAIgGiVBlupQ8H6P4QqAcX7FktHvlrfe0mFEjZerE0fxG4LFXta3wzMSq5Ozkmh8Y-hN-EvMuV7twE7L3KAzDP-35CTOGFMO_DlWo1i7wxQTYUzzpYA8n8qhqIpgGxFSC8UBfyR9bHthyZN8y51_RGwOHVDVx2Okk7ti-00QtGFRtGljE3WC3muGPQLuxGywzEwprGmrDX9tMwZQO1VRj2s6d1ZB6ooqqfrl5ybM_uvSYA_wRvHdToftOxTOTAQrIbV43J1Cq3I-3Vj39UdvNGx4hEA_IZYvBKjRK4k_MC_SxAq8CS3bWvKObFFEbzLdgSurQ5i0SALGXBRhtoojuug_MoUgRzp70sIfG2gUqI4yyIUmAwBeUdI3xD1wDwXIe3My5b_P4XJpGI1FKJHgkFiE3lC0zExIvwxK9FX7Ewld8JIR4o0SfzmlwrXepSp6JfNilqPQcjLwD0lR_ifojuKWRxtH22IX0iVvP3opGnGTzmhn5aX-oGrjYsQ6cvNf2GoZb74PDvg_aJ70zRHKzyeH4nrp-dKaVzaK3pNXYfgiL34uoBh3TGojVHTU3sSlbn5g63bl4twQXyxzT-87obURa93HNllsdY9ygKBdnwJmNOkqPBBn56zxy4_b4ZiB0O8xvHp1w4S2liR___eetn0diBHrebS_zS5mw3Dh0ECyo35rNN48rFBnhChhklNx1KCz6Xn5YnrdkqaUdUychIwHlXW4TyFNxXRpbi4NzOzYK9fPkkJ5LnopaMgHkhUTRw5phJY2zZfjZGmmFvEg65f9oMhe01CEdP1KBeNWPyfJuzHj9s3LoE-SaeiY_OY-u0Le_Z22mO72MgY3"},{"type":"message","id":"msg_0bef3fb5108a93b4016adb1f28ee9081918752203259224fc3","role":"assistant","status":"completed","content":[{"type":"output_text","text":"{\n  \"status\": \"ROUTINE_COMPLETION_CANDIDATE\",\n  \"detailed_assessment\": \"The reported lower-bound obstacle can be completed by ordinary rigorous expansion along the supplied arbitrary-cover and slicing route; it does not require a new core argument. The key point is that enlarging B_i to J_i does preserve the needed fiber cover: for each t, the indices with t in B_i already cover E, and B_i⊆J_i. The integrated inequality can therefore be justified for every t, even though the original B_i need not be measurable. Zero-diameter members contribute zero when s>0, and the enlargement errors can be chosen with arbitrarily small weighted sum. The scale conclusion follows quantitatively by comparing scale contents at every δ, not by an informal limit. For the positive-D case it is enough to use 0<s<D, avoiding any 0^0 convention. The upper-cover argument also needs routine elaboration for zero-diameter cover members. No substantive implication remains unsupported in these passages.\",\n  \"corrected_proof\": \"Use c_a(A,δ) for the infimum of Σ_i diam(W_i)^a over countable covers of A by sets W_i with diameters at most δ. Hausdorff measure is the limit of these contents as δ decreases to zero, up to the fixed positive normalization, which does not affect dimension. Write D=dim_H(E) and F=E×[0,1] with the specified concatenated Euclidean metric.\\n\\nFor the upper bound, fix q>D. The standard dimension-threshold characterization gives H^q(E)=0, hence for every δ>0 and ε>0 there is a countable cover (U_j) of E with diameters r_j≤δ and Σ_j r_j^q<ε. Empty members can be discarded. Replace each singleton member (diameter zero) by a ball covering it of positive diameter b_j≤δ, choosing the b_j so that the sum of their qth powers is less than an additional arbitrary ε. Retain all positive-diameter members. Thus we may use a cover with positive diameters r_j≤δ and total q-cost arbitrarily small. Take δ≤1. For each j, cover [0,1] by at most 1/r_j+1 intervals of length at most r_j. Their products with U_j cover U_j×[0,1]. Each such product has Euclidean diameter at most √2 r_j, since each factor's diameter is at most r_j. The total (q+1)-cost of these product covers is at most 2(√2)^(q+1)Σ_j r_j^q. Their diameters are at most √2δ. Since the original ε and the extra replacement cost can be arbitrarily small, H^(q+1)(F)=0. Thus dim_H(F)≤q+1 for every q>D, and dim_H(F)≤D+1.\\n\\nSuppose D>0 and fix any s with 0<s<D. The threshold characterization gives H^s(E)=∞, so c_s(E,δ) tends to infinity as δ decreases to zero. Fix 0<δ≤1 and any countable cover (V_i) of F with ρ_i=diam(V_i)≤δ. Empty members may be discarded. Let A_i and B_i be the coordinate projections of V_i onto R^n and R. Projection does not increase diameter, so diam(A_i)≤ρ_i and diam(B_i)≤ρ_i. For each i choose an open interval J_i containing B_i of length at most ρ_i+η_i, where η_i>0. Such an interval exists because B_i is bounded and has diameter at most ρ_i. Choose the errors so that Σ_i η_iρ_i^s<ε; for example, after enumerating the cover, take η_i=ε2^(-i)/(1+ρ_i^s). For any t∈[0,1], the sets A_i for which t∈B_i cover E: for each x∈E, (x,t) belongs to some V_i. Since B_i⊆J_i, the sets A_i for which t∈J_i also cover E. Their diameters are at most δ, and therefore, for every t∈[0,1],\\n\\nc_s(E,δ) ≤ Σ_i 1_{J_i}(t)ρ_i^s.\\n\\nThe right side is a measurable nonnegative sum. Integrating over [0,1] and applying Tonelli gives\\n\\nc_s(E,δ) ≤ Σ_i |J_i∩[0,1]|ρ_i^s ≤ Σ_i (ρ_i+η_i)ρ_i^s ≤ Σ_i ρ_i^(s+1)+ε.\\n\\nThis includes ρ_i=0: since s>0 its weighted contribution is zero. Let ε decrease to zero. The resulting inequality holds for every countable product cover of scale δ. Taking the infimum over those covers yields c_s(E,δ)≤c_(s+1)(F,δ). Since c_s(E,δ) tends to infinity as δ decreases to zero, so does c_(s+1)(F,δ); thus H^(s+1)(F)=∞ and dim_H(F)≥s+1. This holds for every 0<s<D, giving dim_H(F)≥D+1.\\n\\nIf D=0, choose x₀∈E. The coordinate projection F→[0,1] is 1-Lipschitz for the stated Euclidean metrics and is onto because (x₀,t)∈F for every t∈[0,1]. The standard Lipschitz dimension inequality and dim_H([0,1])=1 give dim_H(F)≥1=D+1. This also covers n=0. Together with the upper bound this proves the target.\",\n  \"unresolved_points\": [\n    \"For a fully sourced proof or NL0, verify the exact pinned signatures and applicability of the Hausdorff-dimension threshold facts: H^q(E)=0 for q>dim_H(E), and H^s(E)=∞ for 0<s<dim_H(E). The content-limit consequence used above follows directly from the Hausdorff-measure definition.\",\n    \"For the D=0 case, verify the standard Lipschitz nonincrease theorem for Hausdorff dimension and the dimension-one fact for [0,1], with their exact source statements.\",\n    \"The upper-bound choice q>D requires D<∞. This follows from the standard finite-dimensional Euclidean ambient dimension bound (or its Hausdorff-measure consequence); its exact source/application should be verified in a complete sourced proof.\",\n    \"Tonelli for the nonnegative measurable indicator series is the only integration theorem needed in the slicing passage. No measurability of B_i is assumed.\"\n  ]\n}","annotations":[],"logprobs":[]}],"phase":"final_answer"}],"error":null,"incomplete_details":null,"tools":[{"type":"function","name":"search_mathlib","description":"在固定 mathlib 的 Lean 源码中搜索正则表达式，返回至多 100 行。","parameters":{"type":"object","properties":{"query":{"type":"string"}},"required":["query"],"additionalProperties":false},"output_schema":null,"strict":false},{"type":"function","name":"read_mathlib","description":"读取固定 mathlib 中一个 Mathlib/...lean 文件的指定行。","parameters":{"type":"object","properties":{"path":{"type":"string"},"start_line":{"type":"integer"},"line_count":{"type":"integer"}},"required":["path","start_line","line_count"],"additionalProperties":false},"output_schema":null,"strict":false},{"type":"function","name":"read_task_input","description":"Read an explicitly frozen task document by ID; no other local files are accessible.","parameters":{"type":"object","properties":{"id":{"type":"string"},"start":{"type":"integer"},"end":{"type":"integer"}},"required":["id","start","end"],"additionalProperties":false},"output_schema":null,"strict":false},{"type":"function","name":"read_yui","description":"Read declared Yui rules/templates; no tests, historical runs or personal files.","parameters":{"type":"object","properties":{"path":{"type":"string"},"start":{"type":"integer"},"end":{"type":"integer"}},"required":["path","start","end"],"additionalProperties":false},"output_schema":null,"strict":false},{"type":"function","name":"check_artifact","description":"Check a JSON artifact against the explicit stage contract; this is not automatic mathematical certification.","parameters":{"type":"object","properties":{"artifact_json":{"type":"string"}},"required":["artifact_json"],"additionalProperties":false},"output_schema":null,"strict":false},{"type":"function","name":"submit_artifact","descri